<!--
  Sitemap hygiene. Four rules, all easy to break:

  0. (L-29) THIS COMMENT MUST STAY VALID XML: no two hyphens in a row anywhere in
     it, and no angle brackets. From 06/08 to 15/09/2026 a git command line with
     double-hyphen flags sat here, the XML parser stopped at it, and Google read the
     rest (it quoted a meta tag, a link tag and a head tag) as a page: Search Console
     reported "Sitemap is HTML" with 0 discovered pages. test/sitemap.test.js refuses
     both characters now.
  1. (L-07) ONLY indexable pages belong here. `termos` and `privacidade` carry a
     robots meta tag with noindex and were listed anyway until Aug 2026, asking a
     crawler to fetch a page that then tells it to go away. The 404 page is out for
     the same reason. If a page gains a noindex, remove it from this file in the
     SAME delivery.
  2. (L-07) `lastmod` is the date THAT page's content last changed, updated by hand
     in the delivery that changes the page. It is not a build timestamp and must
     never be bumped globally: a sitemap that claims every page changed today
     teaches the crawler to ignore the field. The date of the last commit that
     touched the page file (git log, one entry, short date) is the honest value.
  3. (L-23) Every loc names the address that answers 200: WITHOUT `.html`. The static
     assets serve /blog/slug and answer the `.html` form with a 307, so listing it
     spends crawl budget on a temporary redirect. The same address is each page's
     `canonical`; test/sitemap.test.js fails when either side names the other form.
     Dropping the extension in Sep 2026 moved no `lastmod`: an address is not content.

  L-16: the English version is listed as its own URL. The PT-BR/EN pairing is
  declared by the reciprocal hreflang alternate links in each page's head, which is
  what Google reads; keeping it out of here keeps this file simple.
-->
